course: dictionary
lesson: 7055
title: Pasteurise
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To pasteurise a drink or food, like milk or juice, means to heat it to a set temperature for a short time, to kill harmful germs, and then cool it. «Milk is cooled at the farm, carried by tanker to a dairy and heated briefly to kill germs, which is called pasteurising after Louis Pasteur, who studied germs in the 1800s.» The clue is «to kill germs»: the heating is what stops the germs.
explain: Pasteurise changes its ending: pasteurises, pasteurised and pasteurising are all forms of the same word. «Pasteurised milk keeps about a week in the fridge.» Here pasteurised describes the milk, which has already been heated. Labels say «pasteurised» or «unpasteurised», so the label tells you whether the milk has been heated.
explain: Context can also show you a difference. «UHT milk, heated much hotter for a few seconds, keeps for months on a shelf until it is opened.» Both pasteurised and UHT milk are heated to kill germs, but UHT is heated much hotter, for a shorter time, and lasts much longer.
fact: Pasteurise means to heat a drink or food for a short time, to kill harmful germs, and then cool it.
fact: Pasteurising is named after the French scientist Louis Pasteur.
fact: Pasteurised milk is usually heated to about 72°C for 15 seconds.
fact: «Pasteurised» milk keeps about a week in the fridge, while «UHT» milk keeps for months on a shelf until it is opened.
fact: In the 1800s, Louis Pasteur and Robert Koch proved that germs cause many diseases, and pasteurised milk helped to save millions of lives.
exercise: «Milk is pasteurised to kill harmful germs.» What does pasteurise mean? | To add sugar to juice | *To heat a drink or food for a short time to kill harmful germs, then cool it | To freeze a drink solid for months | To shake milk until it froths
exercise: Which word on a label means the milk has been heated to kill germs? | cooled | tanker | *pasteurised | unpasteurised
exercise: Who is pasteurising named after? | Your teacher | Isaac Newton | *Louis Pasteur | Robert Koch
test: «UHT milk, heated much hotter for a few seconds, keeps for months.» How is UHT milk different from pasteurised milk? | *It is heated much hotter, but for a shorter time | It is frozen before it is sold | It is heated cooler, for a longer time | It is never heated at all
test: How long does pasteurised milk keep in the fridge? | Only while it is on the shelf | A day | Forever | *About a week
exam: Why do food makers pasteurise milk? | To make it thicker | To make it colder for ever | *To kill harmful germs so it is safer to drink | To make it taste of chocolate
